==English==
{{was wotd|2025|December|13}}

===Pronunciation===
{{multiple images
|image1 = Yarn skeins.JPG
|caption1 = Skeins ''(etymology 1, noun {{senseno|en|gathering of yarn etc}})'' of [[yarn#Noun|yarn]].
|image2 = A Skein of Geese - geograph.org.uk - 1571655.jpg
|caption2 = A skein ''(etymology 1, noun {{senseno|en|group of wildfowl}})'' of [[goose#Noun|geese]].
}}

* {{enPR|skān|a=RP,GA}}, {{IPA|en|/skeɪn/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-skein.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Naomi Persephone Amethyst (NaomiAmethyst)-skein.wav|a=GA}}
* {{rhymes|en|eɪn|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*skend-}}
The {{lg|noun}} is derived from {{inh|en|enm|skaine}}, {{m|enm|skayne|t=quantity of string, thread, etc., wound on a reel; the string, thread, etc., so wound}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=skaine|pos=n|id=MED40605}}</ref> from {{der|en|fro|escaigne}} (modern {{cog|fr|écagne}}, {{m|fr|écaigne}} {{qualifier|Picardy}}); further etymology uncertain,<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=1196329536|date=September 2025|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref> probably from {{der|en|cel-pro|-}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*skend-|t=to split off}}.

The {{lg|verb}} is derived from noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=2815021853|date=September 2025}}</ref>

{{col-top|2|cognates}}
* {{cog|ga|scáinne|t=skein, clew}}
{{col-bottom}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|gathering of yarn etc}} A [[quantity]] of [[thread#Noun|thread]], [[yarn#Noun|yarn]], etc., [[wind#Verb|wound]] on a [[reel#Noun|reel]] then [[remove#Verb|removed]] and [[loosely]] [[knot#Verb|knotted]] into an [[oblong#Adjective|oblong]] [[shape#Noun|shape]]; a skein of [[cotton#Noun|cotton]] is [[form#Verb|formed]] by [[eighty]] [[turn#Noun|turns]] of thread around a reel with a [[fifty-four]] [[inch#Noun|inch]] [[diameter]].
#: {{coordinate terms|en|ball|cake|hank}}
#* {{RQ:Skelton Poetical Works|volume=I|poem=Elynour Rummyng|lines=309–311|page=105|passage=Some for very nede / Layde downe a '''skeyne''' of threde, / And some a '''skeyne''' of yarne; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Skelton Poetical Works|volume=I|poem=Garland|lines=798–800|page=393|passage=Reche me that '''skane''' of tewly sylk; / And, Wynde me that botowme of such an hew, / Grene, rede, tawny, whyte, blak, purpill, and blew.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Taming of the Shrew|act=IV|scene=iii|page=224|column=1|passage=Brau'd in mine ovvne houſe vvith a '''skeine''' of thred: / Avvay thou Ragge, thou quantitie, thou remnant, / Or I ſhall ſo be-mete thee vvith thy yard, / As thou ſhalt thinke on prating vvhil'ſt thou liu'ſt: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Troilus and Cressida Q1|act=V|scene=i|page=79|passage=No vvhy art thou then exaſperate, thou idle immaterial '''skiene''' of ſleiue ſilke, thou greene ſacenet flap for a ſore eye, thou toſſell of a Prodigals purſe—thou {{...}}|translation=No, why art thou then exasperate, thou idle immaterial '''skein''' of sleeve silk, thou greene sarsenet flap for a sore eye, thou tassel of a prodigal's purse—thou {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Davenant Love and Honour|page=10|column=2|passage=VVhat is ſhe heire to? a braſſe thimble, and / A '''skeane''' of brovvn thread?}}
#* {{RQ:Defoe Giving Alms|page=16|passage=Suppoſe novv a VVork-houſe for Employment of Poor Children, ſets them to ſpinning of VVorſted.—For every '''Skein''' of VVorſted theſe Poor Children Spin, there muſt be a '''Skein''' the leſs Spun by ſome poor Family or Perſon that ſpun it before; {{...}} 'tis only the tranſpoſing the Manufacture from ''Colcheſter'' to ''London'', and taking the Bread out of the Mouths of the Poor of ''Eſſex'' to put it into the Mouths of the Poor of ''Middleſex''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1798|author=John Ebers|entry=Knouel|title=The New and Complete Dictionary of the German and English Languages{{nb...|Composed Chiefly after the German Dictionaries of Mr. [Johann Christoph] Adelung and of Mr. [Christian Friedrich] Schwan. Every German Word being Rendered into Proper English and Thoroughly Enriched with Phrases and Terms of Arts and Sciences: A Work, which will be Useful and Even Indispensible, and therefore Welcome to All Such as have a Mind to Translate or Read the Works of Either of the Two Languages, Elaborated by John Ebers.}}|location=Leipzig, Saxony|publisher={{...|Printed for}} [[w:Breitkopf & Härtel|Brietkopf and Haertel]]|volume=!II (H–R of the German Alphabet Explained in English)|page=360|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/bub_gb_4-9IAAAAcAAJ/page/360/mode/1up|column=1|oclc=993929329|passage=''Knouel, der, oder Kna̔uel'', a Bottom or Clevv of Thread or of Yarn, a Hank, a '''Skain''' of Yarn or Thread.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1807|author=[[w:George Gregory (British writer)|G[eorge] Gregory]]|entry=ROPE-MAKING|title=A Dictionary of Arts and Sciences.{{nb...|[...] In Two Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher={{...|Printed [by]}} [T. Gillet] for [[w:Richard Phillips (publisher)|Richard Phillips]],{{nb...|No. 6, Bridge-Street, Blackfriars.}}|volume=II|pages=602–603|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dictionaryofarts02greg/page/603/mode/1up|oclc=1547526516|passage=The aim of the rope-maker is to unite the strings of a great number of fibres. {{...}} This is done by twisting or twining them together, which causes them to compress each other. {{...}} Consequently, if we pull at this twisted '''skain''', we shall not separate it by drawing one parcel out from among the rest, but the whole fibres will break; and if the distribution of the fibres has been very equable, the '''skain''' will be nearly of the same strength in every part.}}
#* {{RQ:Scott Antiquary|volume=I|chapter=I|page=4|passage=[A] sharp-looking old dame, {{...}} inhabited a "laigh {{quote-gloss|low}} shop," ''anglicé'' {{quote-gloss|in English}}, a cellar, opening to the High-street by a strait and steep stair, at the bottom of which she sold tape, thread, needles, '''skeans''' of worsted, coarse linen cloth, and such feminine gear, to those who had the courage and skill to descend to the profundity of her dwelling, without falling headlong themselves, or throwing down any of the numerous articles which, piled on each side of the descent, indicated the profession of the trader below.}}
#* {{RQ:Wilde Dorian Gray|page=12|passage=“How horribly unjust of you!” cried Lord Henry, tilting his hat back and looking up at the little clouds that, like ravelled '''skeins''' of glossy white silk, were drifting across the hollowed turquoise of the summer sky.}}
#* {{RQ:Woolf Voyage Out|chapter=XV|page=245|passage=The embroidery, which was a matter for thought, the design being difficult and the colours wanting consideration, brought lapses into the dialogue when she seemed to be engrossed in her '''skeins''' of silk, or, with head a little drawn back and eyes narrowed, considered the effect of the whole.}}
#* {{RQ:Christie Mysterious Affair|year=1927|page=180|passage="He did have a beard, sir," replied Dorcas, smiling. "And well I know it, for he borrowed two '''skeins''' of my black wool to make it with! And I’m sure it looked wonderfully natural at a distance.{{nb...}}"}}
#* {{RQ:Yeats Winding Stair|year=1933|poem=IV. Crazy Jane and Jack the Journeyman|stanza=1|page=66|passage=The more I leave the door unlatched / The sooner love is gone, / For love is but a '''skein''' unwound / Between the dark and dawn.|footer=A figurative use.}}
#* {{RQ:T. S. Eliot Murder|page=26|passage=You hold the '''skein''': wind, Thomas, wind / The thread of eternal life and death. / You hold this power, hold it.|footer=A figurative use.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} A [[thing]] [[resemble|resembling]] a skein ''(noun {{senseno|en|gathering of yarn etc}})'' of thread, yarn, etc.
## {{lb|en|ichthyology}} The [[membrane]] of a [[fish#Noun|fish]] [[ovary]].
## {{senseid|en|group of wildfowl}}{{lb|en|UK|dialectal|ornithology|collective}} A [[group#Noun|group]] of [[wildfowl]] (for example, [[goose#Noun|geese]] or [[swan#Noun|swans]]) in [[flight#Noun|flight]].
##: {{coordinate terms|en|gaggle|wedge}}
##* {{quote-web|en|date=6 June 2019|title={{w|Newsround}}: A Gaggle, a Confusion and a Conspiracy&nbsp;– Bizarre Animal Collective Group Names|work={{w|CBBC}}, {{w|BBC}}|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250814135747/https://www.bbc.co.uk/newsround/48539466|passage=A group of geese is called a gaggle. {{...}} They're only referred to as a gaggle when they're on land. When they're flying in formation they can be referred to as a '''skein'''.|brackets=on}}
## {{lb|en|obsolete|biochemistry|cytology|also|attributive}} {{synonym of|en|spireme|t=the [[tangled#Adjective|tangled]] [[mass#Noun|mass]] of [[strand#Noun|strands]] of [[chromatin]] [[see#Verb|seen]] in the [[early#Adjective|early]] [[stage#Noun|stages]] of [[mitosis]], originally [[believe]]d to be a [[single#Adjective|single]] [[continuous]] strand (or [[two#Numeral|two]] in a [[diploid#Adjective|diploid]] [[cell#Noun|cell]], etc.)}}.
# {{lb|en|figurative}}
## A [[tangle#Noun|tangle]], a [[weave#Noun|weave]], a [[web#Noun|web]].
##* {{RQ:Jonson Staple of News|scene=ii|page=68|passage=Not that I ſee through his perplexed plots, / And hidden ends, nor that my parts depend / Vpon the vnvvinding this ſo knotted '''skeane''', / Doe I beſeech your patience.}}
##* {{RQ:Cowper Task|page=98|passage=They diſentangle from the puzzled '''ſkein''' / In vvhich obſcurity has vvrapp'd them up, / The threads of politic and ſhrevvd deſign / That ran through all his purposes, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Keats Endymion|lines=760–763|page=141|passage=But first a little patience; first undo / This tangled thread, and wind it to a clue. / Ah, gentle! 'tis as weak as spider's '''skein'''; / And shouldst thou break it—What, is it done so clean?}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Tales of My Landlord 4|volume=III|page=132|passage={{...}} Achilles Tatius, with a feeling of much insecurity, awaited the unwinding of the perilous '''skein''' of state politics, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Hardy Far from the Madding Crowd|volume=II|chapter=Eventide—a Second Declaration|page=256|passage=This evening Bathsheba was unusually excited, her red cheeks and lips contrasting lustrously with the mazy '''skeins''' of her shadowy hair.}}
##* {{RQ:Hardy Tess|volume=I|chapter=I|page=75|passage=He watched her pretty and unconscious munching through the '''skeins''' of smoke that pervaded the tent, and Tess Durbeyfield did not divine, as she innocently looked down at the roses in her bosom, that there behind the blue narcotic haze sat the 'tragic mischief' of her drama—he who was to be the blood-red ray in the spectrum of her young life.}}
##* {{RQ:Wells Ann Veronica|edition=US|page=307|passage=But when she turned her thoughts to Morningside Park she perceived the tangled '''skein''' of life was now to be further complicated by his romantic importunity.}}
##* {{RQ:Doyle Case-Book of Sherlock Holmes|year=1927|page=208|passage=The practical application of what I have said is very close to the problem which I am investigating. It is a tangled '''skein''', you understand, and I am looking for a loose end.}}
##* {{quote-book|en|year=1964|author=w:Issac Asimov|chapter=How to Use this Book|title=[[w:Asimov's Biographical Encyclopedia of Science and Technology|Asimov’s Biographical Encyclopedia of Science and Technology{{nb...|The Lives and Achievements of 1195 Great Scientists from Ancient Times to the Present Chronologically Arranged: By Isaac Asimov: New Revised Edition}}]]|edition=revised|location=Garden City, N.Y.|publisher=[[w:Doubleday (publisher)|Doubleday & Company]]|year_published=1972|page=vii|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/biographicalency0000unse_k8o8/page/n10/mode/1up|oclc=277910|passage=[S]cience is a complex '''skein''', intricately interknotted across the artificial boundaries we draw only that we may the more easily encompass its parts in our mind. Pick up any thread of that '''skein''' and the whole structure will follow.}}
##* {{RQ:NYT|date=4 January 2022|author=w:Mark Landler|title=Prince Andrew’s uncertain legal fate casts shadow on Britain’s royals|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20250731230446/https://www.nytimes.com/2022/01/04/world/europe/prince-andrew-sexual-abuse-royal-family.html|passage=It was the latest in a '''skein''' of legal maneuvers by the prince {{quote-gloss|[[w:Andrew Mountbatten-Windsor|Prince Andrew, Duke of York]]}}'s lawyers to defuse Ms. [[w:Virginia Giuffre|{{quote-gloss|Virginia}} Giuffre]]'s case.}}
## {{lb|en|sports}} A [[winning streak]].
## {{lb|en|US|radio|television|dated}} A [[series]] created by a {{l|en|web#Noun|t=[[major#Adjective|major]] [[broadcasting#Noun|broadcasting]] [[network#Noun|network]]}}.
##* {{quote-journal|en|date=4 March 1950|title=Three Tele Nets All-out to Grab Frigidaire Show|editor=Roger S. Littleford Jr.|journal=[[w:Billboard (magazine)|The Billboard: The World’s Foremost Amusement Weekly]]|location=Cincinnati, Oh.|publisher=Roger S. Littleford Jr.; William D. Littleford|volume=62|issue=9|page=5|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=WykEAAAAMBAJ&pg=PT4|column=3|oclc=7524629|passage=All three tele '''skeins''' are pitching furiously to snag the super Easter Day tele show to be bankrolled by Frigidaire, but no decision has been reached at press time. {{...}} Bankroller will buy time on the '''skein''' delivering the largest number of live coaxial outlets, but none of the webs are as yet able to make definite commitments.}}
##* {{quote-journal|en|year=1963|journal=Radio Television Daily|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Scheuer Publications|volume=93|page=5|column=4|oclc=4836269|passage=Three comedy shows from the U.S. are in the CTV lineup: CBS-TV's {{w|Phil Silvers}} and {{w|Danny Thomas}} '''skeins''' and NBC-TV's "{{w|Harry's Girls}}."}}

